**Flag codes (closed list; the property first: a flag states a fact about a row that the sources print; at least these; any other flag: prove it meets the property or STOP):** `REGISTRY_ABSENT` (the row's registry cell is `None`), `M106_NO_SM_ITEM` (row M106 only), `NO_VENDOR_ITEM` (the row has no vendor item), `VENDOR_ITEM_FROM_SEED_ONLY` (an absent row whose vendor id comes from the seed alone), `PR85_CATALOGUE_ONLY` (an absent row whose vendor id sits on a catalogue-only map entry), `SHARED_CONTROL` (its registry control serves more than one row), `MULTI_ITEM_ROW` (the row takes more than one map entry), `SUB_SETTING_ROW` (a vendor item carries sub-setting ids on this row), `DUP_NAME_ITEM` (a vendor item is one of the 26 duplicate-name items), `SEED_DIFFERS` (the seed names an id the row does not carry), `SEED_SILENT` (the seed names none, the row carries a vendor item), `NO_ITEM_KEY` (a registry control on the row whose entries carry no `item_key`).
**`resolved_by` (closed list):** `one_entry`, `seed`, `item_key_tail`, `elimination`, `spans_entries`, `hand_row`, `absent_seed`, `absent_none`.

## THE RESOLUTION RULES (binding; each row takes the first rule that applies; any row no rule resolves is a STOP, never a guess)
- **R0 (the key).** Row n of both seed files is template row `M<n:03d>`; prove the equality of FACT 6 for all 132 and STOP on any other.
- **R1 (registry).** The row's registry control is the one `UQ-nnn` of its `map132.txt` line, or null for `None`. A line with two or more `UQ-` tokens is a STOP (`TWO_REGISTRY_CONTROLS`): no rule exists for it. The set of absent rows is derived from `map132.txt` and proved equal to the set derived from `map132_sm316.txt`.
- **R2 (absent rows).** `registry_control` null. If the seed names an SM id: `vendor_items` is that one id with no sub-settings, `resolved_by` `absent_seed`, and `pr85_control` is the control id of the map entry whose `how` is `catalogue-only` and whose `sm_item` is that id, if there is one (then `PR85_CATALOGUE_ONLY`, else `VENDOR_ITEM_FROM_SEED_ONLY`). If it names none: `vendor_items` empty, `absent_none`, `NO_VENDOR_ITEM`. **One hand row, H-1:** M008 names a sub-setting of `ILUnifiedPassword` (the gap analysis, section 5 item 9; the map's reason on `UQ-008`, both RELAYED): its `vendor_items` is `[{ILUnifiedPassword, [PLAutoEWSAdminPasswordReset]}]`, `resolved_by` `hand_row`, `SUB_SETTING_ROW`; prove by program that the catalogue holds that sub-setting id under that item and that its `name` is the vendor's "Use Random EWS Password" (the drafter's lookup found exactly one such sub-setting; a planted wrong id must be reported). **M106 is flagged `M106_NO_SM_ITEM` besides `REGISTRY_ABSENT` and `NO_VENDOR_ITEM`.**
- **R3 (a registry control).** `E(c)` is the list of map entries whose `control_id` is `c`. No entry: STOP. One entry: take it (`one_entry`). Several entries and the control is on one row only: take all of them (`spans_entries`, `MULTI_ITEM_ROW`). Several entries and the control is on several rows `U(c)` (`SHARED_CONTROL` on each): for each row in order, (a) if exactly one entry's `sm_item` equals the row's seed id, take it (`seed`); else (b) if exactly one entry's `item_key`, cut at its last dot, equals the row's template `setting` after `normaliseName`, take it (`item_key_tail`); else (c) if exactly one row of `U(c)` is still unresolved and exactly one entry of `E(c)` is still untaken, take it (`elimination`); else STOP. (Rule (a) is tried on all rows of `U(c)` before (b), and (b) before (c).)
- **R4 (policy items).** `policy_item_keys` are the `item_key` of the entries taken; if the row has a registry control and none of its entries carries an `item_key`: `NO_ITEM_KEY`.
- **R5 (the seed cross-check).** `seed_vendor_id` is the seed's id or null; `SEED_DIFFERS` when it is non-null and not among the row's vendor item ids; `SEED_SILENT` when it is null and the row has a vendor item. `DUP_NAME_ITEM` and `SUB_SETTING_ROW` follow from the catalogue and the entries. **The sample's `setting` text is used ONLY as the key of R0 and rule (b), inside your program, never written to any output.**
The drafter's prototype resolved all 132 rows with these rules and no STOP (FACT 8); your own program must reach the same resolution, which you show by printing, per `resolved_by`, the count and the M-ids.

## THE REQUIRED QUESTION (stamp checklist item 11): the crosswalk claims to cover EVERY sample row and EVERY catalogue item. Three things the sources hold that are NOT a clean one-to-one row, and what this kit does with each
1. **Duplicate vendor names: 13 names on 26 items.** A name match alone is wrong for them: the seed's own SM-id column picked the Cloud twin on row 36. **Covered, by flag, not a limit:** each row takes the map's hand-resolved on-device item (rule R3 over the map's `hand` entries); `DUP_NAME_ITEM` marks every row whose vendor item sits in the duplicate set (the drafter's prototype found 13 such on-device items on rows), `SEED_DIFFERS` marks rows 36 and 114 (and every other row where the seed names a different id), and each of the 13 Cloud twins appears once in `catalogue_items` with `NO_SAMPLE_ROW`, `DUPLICATE_NAME_TWIN` and its peer.
2. **Catalogue items no sample row maps to: 18 of the 143 (FACT 8: the 13 Cloud twins and 5 others, of which the gap analysis names three as items the sample omits).** The crosswalk is sample-row first, so it CANNOT claim a sample row for each item. **Covered as a limit, stated in the README and in `_meta.counts`:** every one of the 143 appears once in `catalogue_items`, with its `sample_rows` (possibly empty) and `NO_SAMPLE_ROW`. "Every catalogue item" means every item is LISTED with its rows or its flag, not that every item has a row.
3. **Sub-settings.** The catalogue holds 376, the sample names items; a row is a sub-setting of an item only in 6 rows (8, 114, 115, 116, 117, 118; FACT 7), and 8 sub-settings of 376 are named by a row (7 by the map's entries, 1 by the hand row H-1). **Covered by listing, stated as a limit:** all 376 ids are carried under their parent item in `catalogue_items` (`sub_setting_ids`), the named ones in `sub_settings_named` with their rows; the rest are "not named by the sample", which is a fact about the sample, not a gap of the crosswalk. Also stated as limits (not in the three): 7 registry controls on no row; 6 shared-item sub-controls whose map entries are on no row; rows 80 and 106 with no vendor item; M008's sub-setting is a hand row (H-1), not a name match in the sources.

